WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.525
Welcome back to 8.701.

00:00:10.525 --> 00:00:13.700
Our second part of
the chapter on QCD

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.650
is on elastic
electron-proton scattering.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:22.600
So elastic electron-proton
scattering in general

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.100
has a long history, going
back into the 1960s.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:31.750
Very famous examples are the
MIT-SLAC or SLAC-MIT experiment

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.820
which led to the
Nobel Prize in physics

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:38.270
for Jerry Friedman, Henry
Kendall, and Richard Taylor

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.960
in 1990.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.720
And the most recent
and highest energetic

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.750
electron-positron experiments
were conducted at DESY

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:51.640
in Hamburg, at the
so-called HERA ring.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.640
And we'll go back into
those experiments.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.990
They are famous for not just
elastic electron-positron

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:00.850
scattering, but
inelastic and deep

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.440
inelastic electron-positron
scattering,

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.600
which is going to be
subject of future lectures.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.060
What we are trying to do here--

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:15.010
you might still wonder why
this is topic of a QCD lecture.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:18.340
What we're trying to do is
study the structure of protons.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:23.410
And the way to look at this
is in these Feynman diagrams,

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:26.950
we're using this photon here
to probe into the structure

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.230
of the proton.

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:32.050
We can start from this
scattering process, which

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.410
we already calculated,
and we derived the Mott

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:38.410
scattering
cross-section formula.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:42.220
But we really want to reconsider
this proton now as a prop.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:45.710
In elastic scattering, we are
not destroying the proton,

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.665
so we leave the proton intact
in the scattering process.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:55.490
We do know that the proton
is not a point-like particle.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.580
So if you want to
now study the proton,

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.440
we have to take into
account that it's

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.660
built out of constituents
and that it has an extension.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:04.790
It's not point-like anymore.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.840
And one way to do
this is by analyzing

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.140
the Fourier transform of the
charge density functions.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:15.080
Remember, the photon couples
to charged particles.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.430
And so when we use the
photon to probe a proton,

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:22.460
it probes the charge
distribution inside the proton.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:27.640
So we build a Fourier transform
of the charge distribution,

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:33.370
and then can extend
the cross-section

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:37.330
from a point-like cross-section
via this Fourier transform

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.430
of this charge distribution.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:40.360
Great.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.240
In electron-positron scattering,
there is another point.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.580
It's not just the
extension of the proton,

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.650
but also the fact that
the proton carries recoil.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.790
And so we need to use
two form factors in order

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:56.820
to describe the
cross-section fully.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.470
So let's have a look
at the amplitude.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.390
Again, we can start
from where we left off

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.410
with the electron-positron
scattering,

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:08.730
or electron-muon scattering, and
write down our matrix elements,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.680
and just now for the proton.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.880
What we do here is we modify
the vertex of the proton.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.520
And the modification is
parameterized in those two

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.260
factors, describing
the cross-section

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.060
of the matrix
elements' amplitude

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.460
with two form factors.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.450
The one, which just looks like
a modification of our spin-1/2

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.590
particle--

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.840
you remember there's
this gamma factor,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.590
and then this is
going to be a number.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.790
And the other one is a
little bit more complicated.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.990
There are the Pauli matrix
here and another number,

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.730
and it's normalized by
the mass of the photon.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:46.230
All right.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:47.730
So this is just a
parameterization.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.147
We haven't done
much physics here.

00:03:49.147 --> 00:03:54.080
We have just parameterized
the distribution.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:00.830
If you then use this, we
can, as I just described,

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:02.740
calculate the
cross-section again,

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.230
using this very same
parameterization,

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:10.390
and get to this formula here
in the laboratory frame.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:16.180
This looks rather complicated,
but if you go back to our form

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:20.529
factor definitions and set this
one to 0 and this one to 1,

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:22.420
you get back-- and
the same here--

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:26.290
we should get back to our
Mott scattering result which

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:28.540
we had before, so
really extended

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:32.560
the discussion to
extended objects,

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.020
and considering the charge
distribution and also

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.700
the recoil in the proton.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.710
So this is great.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:42.300
Historically-- so this
is not a new idea.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.750
This has been done
for generations.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.310
Historically, the
parameterization

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.240
was done slightly different.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.990
And so we introduced the linear
combination of those form

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:53.690
factors, and those
are typically referred

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:56.500
to as the electric and
the magnetic form factors,

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.310
so GE and GM.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.520
This formula here-- and
this is just algebra,

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.340
going from previous
formula to this--

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:11.600
it's called the Rosenbluth
cross-section formula.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:12.100
All right?

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:16.110
So this is just, if you find
this in particle physics

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.240
booklets or in nuclear
physics booklets,

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:19.710
this is what is meant by this.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:24.270
The only thing we did here is
extended the Mott scattering

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.865
formula using extended objects,
extend the charge distribution

00:05:28.865 --> 00:05:32.320
to the [INAUDIBLE]
[? Rosenbluth ?] formula.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:32.820
All right.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:37.810
But we have done that by using a
Fourier transform of the charge

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:38.560
distribution.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.210
So if you now measure
the cross-section,

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:44.800
we can infer the charge
distribution of the proton,

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:48.470
and with that the radius, the
charge radius, of the proton.

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:52.930
This has been done, and
we find that the RMS--

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:54.940
the root mean square--
of the proton charge

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:58.270
is 0.81 femtometers.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:59.500
So that's the charge.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:03.580
And this measured
charge is still today

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.790
a hot topic in particle
physics, because

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:10.960
its measured distributions do
not quite agree with the theory

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:11.890
predictions.

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:14.740
But you see this here
parameterized for this value

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:15.730
of the proton charge.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:18.970
The theory and the global fits
to the data don't quite agree.

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:22.120
You would have to go to slightly
higher values of the proton

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:27.010
radius in order to have theory
agree with the experiments.

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:28.420
So I'll leave it here.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.240
The next lecture now--

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.670
we split this lecture in
two parts, the elastic

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:35.140
and the inelastic scattering.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:38.010
The next lecture will be
on inelastic scattering,

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.960
where we will break the
proton apart and learn

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.740
about the structure
of the proton.

